{"success":true,"markdown":"Few records remain of the terrible war that followed, but its effects are still felt today. Most tales that remain are of great champions who the Prime Deities blessed with their power. Some deities created warriors of their own from light and holy fire. Others imbued fragments of their power into the weapons now called Vestiges of Divergence. More still bestowed blessings upon mortals and called them champions. One tale even tells of a champion blessed by three of the gods in their times of greatest need. Now all but forgotten, this Apotheon was forced by fate into terrible battles across the world.

The sheer magnitude of the energies unleashed in the ensuing battles of gods and mortals frayed the boundaries holding back the elemental chaos, spilling unbridled destruction into the world and bringing utter annihilation. Even the ley lines that direct the flow of magic across Exandria like veins direct blood through the body show signs of being warped by the raw power that the Calamity unleashed. Some well-preserved ruins of the ancient civilizations remain, such as the Drowned City of Cael Morrow in the lands of Marquet, but for the most part, all traces of the old world were erased from the face of Exandria.

So great was the loss of life during the war that even liberal estimates suggest no more than a third of Exandria’s population survived it. This unthinkable devastation inspired some civilizations to flee Exandria entirely and seek refuge on other planes, scattering the Children of Creation across the multiverse. By the war’s end, the only bastion of civilization left on the face of Exandria was the Dawn City itself: Vasselheim.","parentDb":null,"cover":"/images/notion/cover_33923ac1-bdd8-8021-bdae-d4f58be49a8d.webp","icon":null}

